---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
// The essay is mirrored in docs/manifesto.md and its opening paragraph in
// components/landing/ManifestoSection.astro -- edit all three together.
---

<Base
  title="manifesto - embody.tools"
  description="Why TouchDesigner should be transparent, diffable, and built faster alongside your AI agent."
>
  <section class="page-section manifesto-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">manifesto</p>
    <h1>Build faster, in the open</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything is moving faster now. Everyone is shipping. We&rsquo;re entering a brand new
      era of creativity, and Embody exists to help our community build faster and better than
      ever &mdash; open source, no strings attached, nothing SaaSy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <article class="page-section tight manifesto-body">
    <p>
      Most ideas die in the gap between imagining them and seeing them on screen. Trying the
      idea costs more than moving on to something safer, so people move on. Embody is built
      to make trying cheap.
    </p>

    <h2>Forward velocity</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Envoy</strong> is an MCP server that lets your AI assistant work inside your
      live TouchDesigner session. You describe what you want, and the operators appear in
      your network: wired, named, laid out, annotated. Not a screenshot of a network, not a
      code snippet to paste. The real thing, in front of you, ready to play with.
    </p>

    <p>
      The honest numbers: a small change lands in seconds. A complete network takes the agent
      ten to twenty minutes, because it reads your network, plans, builds, and checks its own
      work. What changes is that you don&rsquo;t have to sit through it, and you don&rsquo;t
      have to run just one. Envoy coordinates several AI sessions on the same project. Each
      one claims a part of the network, sees what the others are doing, and is stopped from
      colliding with them. You direct. They build in parallel.
    </p>

    <h2>Lateral velocity</h2>

    <p>
      A tool that only builds forward is a trap. You ask for a thing, you get a thing, and now
      you&rsquo;re stuck with it, because trying something else means starting
      over. <strong>Embody</strong> is the other half. Tag any operator and it lives on disk
      as a file. Branch off the version that was almost right. Restore yesterday&rsquo;s state on
      the next project open. See exactly what changed since last week. Hand the agent
      what&rsquo;s on screen right now and ask for a variation. Every direction you&rsquo;d
      want to move costs about as much as deciding to move there.
    </p>

    <h2>A format the network can live in</h2>

    <p>
      Both halves depend on <strong>TDXN</strong>, a text format the network can live in.
      A <code>.toe</code> file is opaque: your agent can&rsquo;t read it, your diff tool
      can&rsquo;t compare it, and git can&rsquo;t keep two versions of it side by side. TDXN
      writes a network as one YAML file, operators, parameters, connections, layout,
      annotations, DAT content, all of it. With the network in text, compare, revert, and
      branch run at the speed of typing, and a build becomes something you can hand off and
      come back to.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The tool keeps up with you, instead of the other way around.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      That is the whole promise. You stay with the idea. The agents do the translation, in
      parallel.
    </p>

    <p>
      Embody is open source, runs entirely inside TouchDesigner, and works with Claude Code,
      Codex, Gemini CLI, Cursor, Windsurf, GitHub Copilot via VS Code, or any MCP-compatible
      client. Pick an idea you&rsquo;ve been putting off and see how far you get in an hour.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Give the lede the same readable measure as the body and pull it tight to
     the hero so the essay reads as one continuous column. */
  .manifesto-hero .lede {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* The body neutralizes its own top padding, but the hero still carried a full
     section padding-bottom -- a huge lede->body gap. Drop it so the essay flows
     as one column (the body's tight top padding supplies the breathing room). */
  .manifesto-hero {
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* Headings stay lowercase to match the kicker and the site's lowercase voice
     ("see it. embody it."). Scoped to the manifesto's own headings so the body
     prose (TouchDesigner, Envoy, MCP, ...) keeps its proper casing. */
  .manifesto-hero h1 {
    text-transform: lowercase;
  }

  /* Single-column essay capped at a comfortable reading measure (~70ch). The
     body sits directly under the hero, so neutralize the section's own top
     padding and rely on paragraph rhythm instead. */
  .manifesto-body {
    max-width: 70ch;
    padding-top: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
  }

  .manifesto-body p {
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* Section subheads: display font, accent color, generous space above so each
     movement of the argument is clearly set off. */
  .manifesto-body h2 {
    margin: 2.6rem 0 1.1rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 2.4vw, 1.85rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-transform: lowercase;
  }

  .manifesto-body strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .manifesto-body a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .manifesto-body a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-hover);
  }

  .manifesto-body code {
    background: var(--bg-code);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-faint);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 0.05em 0.4em;
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }

  /* Pull quote: an accent rule on the left, display font, larger -- echoes the
     landing page's .pullquote treatment so the two manifesto surfaces feel of a
     piece. */
  .manifesto-body blockquote {
    margin: 2.4rem 0;
    padding: 0 0 0 1.75rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
  }

  .manifesto-body blockquote p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.4vw, 1.85rem);
    line-height: 1.45;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }
</style>
